# GET /start — leave an echo service listening inside the workspace.
#
# `connect` binds a service the workspace is ALREADY running; it never
# starts one. This is the ordinary exec that starts it: a tiny TCP echo
# server on the workspace's loopback at 17777, the port the operator mapped
# to the name `echo` (TXCO_WORKSPACE_SERVICES=echo=17777). It double-forks
# into its own session so it outlives this exec (the local provider kills
# the exec's process group when the command returns), exits by itself after
# ten idle minutes, and is a no-op when something already listens there —
# so /start is safe to call again. On a provider that manages services
# (Sprites), the same thing is a service the workspace declares.
#
# Python is the one interpreter a developer machine reliably has. The server
# is echo_server.py, beside this op: `&include` writes it in as the argument
# string when txco reads the OPS/ tree.
WHEN @src == "http" && @web.req.url.path == "/start"
  EXEC "workspace://tools/exec"
    WITH args = ["python3", "-c", &include("echo_server.py")],
         timeout = "20s",
         into = "_ws"
